{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XiHXsXePpjgmeG4Z1FhRJG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting! So GNU pr uses tab characters as column separators, and it pads columns with tabs to reach the column width. Let me look more carefully.\n\nWith `-2 -t -l 10`:\n- 10 lines per page, -t means no header/footer, so 10 body lines\n- 2 columns means 10 lines per column \n- Fill down: col1 = 1-10, col2 = 11-20\n- Output: `1\\t\\t\\t\\t    11`\n\nWait, that's tabs + spaces. Let me figure out the exact formatting.\n\nThe default width is 72. With 2 columns, each column gets 72/2 = 36 characters.\n\n\nSo the \"1\" needs to be padded to 36 characters using tabs at 8-character stops—one tab gets to position 8, another to 16, then 24, then 32, and finally 4 spaces to reach exactly 36.\n\nNow I'm verifying how this works across multiple columns: with 3 columns and a page width of 72, each column gets 24 characters. The padding uses tabs to reach those boundaries, but the last column doesn't get padded at all. So the algorithm is to divide the page width by the number of columns, then pad each non-final column with tabs and spaces to hit that width. I'm verifying the column width calculation holds up with three columns—each should be 24 characters wide, and I can confirm the tab stops and spacing align correctly so the values land at positions 24 and 48 as expected. Now I need to check how this works with headers included.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":760,"cache_read_input_tokens":27126,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":760},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7fd57023-0c79-4da1-9326-68eed3a3239e","uuid":"f82a204a-6bcf-49ba-b21c-